I am Nick Gillespie with Reason TV today  I'm talking with Alan Gura the lead  lawyer in DC vs. Heller the big DC gun  ban case Alan what are the facts of this  case the facts of this case are fairly  simple but quite stark Washington DC  bans all guns in the home there's a  complete ban on the possession of  handguns and also while they allow you  technically at least in theory to have a  rifle or shotgun rifles and shotguns  have to be at all times unloaded and  either disassembled or bound by trigger  lock there's no exception for using them  for home self-defense so if you happen  to have a shotgun for example in the  house and a bad guy breaks in the only  thing you can do that shotgun is perhaps  whack the intruder over the head with it  or throw it at them but having an  operable firearm in Washington DC is a  misdemeanor why why is this case  important why should people who either  aren't interested in owning a gun or  don't live or do business in DC why  should they care well for many years the  the movement to ban private firearms  ownership in the United States has  advocated a theory that the Second  Amendment doesn't actually guarantee you  any individual right to have a gun  whatsoever and in the absence of an  individual constitutional right to have  a gun  government is then free to regulate gun  ownership out of existence sometimes  they do it as in DC bhaiyya by a Fiat  that simply bans guns and sometimes they  do it with the death of the death of a  thousand cuts taxes regulations a scheme  of regulation that basically makes gun  ownership difficult and not impossible  so for us to establish a constitutional  right would prevent the government from  intruding on people's rights throughout  the country the DC gun laws basically  went into effect in 1976 these ultra  restrictive gun gun owning ownership  regulations what effect did that have on  crime well it's tough to say that the DC  gun ban has improved the crime situation  within 15 years of the gun ban the  murder rate had tripled the  crime rate had gone through the roof if  anything we know that criminals in  Washington DC still have guns still  obtain and use guns readily they are  undeterred by it but law-abiding  individuals are defenseless within their  own homes so you know at best it's done  nothing to stop crime it may have  actually made this situation worse  you're representing a number of  plaintiffs could you talk about two of  them in particular dick Heller whose  name is really the case is known by an  shelly parker what is their story why  did they want to own guns okay Shelly  Parker moved into an improving  neighborhood on Capitol Hill a number of  years ago and she found out the  neighborhood was not improving fast  enough  Shelly became very active in the  community trying to call the police  organize the citizens to get the drug  dealers off the off the block the drug  dealers figured out fairly quickly what  the source of their problems was and  started harassing her she had her  security camera broke and she had a  brick through a window she had a man  drive a car into her fence and in fact  at one point she had one of the drug  dealers try to break into her home  screaming you know [ __ ] I'll kill you I  live here too  Shelly thought I think quite reasonably  that she needs to have a gun to defend  herself the police are not always going  to be able to defend her dick Heller has  an interesting story as well he lives in  a crime wracked neighborhood also on  Capitol Hill and during the day however  he does have a gun he has a gun when he  goes to federal buildings and other city  government buildings as well government  installations where he works as a  security guard he's allowed to have a  gun on the job he is what's called a  special police officer of Washington DC  he has full arrest powers and everything  else while he's on the job and they give  him a gun to do his job but when it's  time to clock out the gun has to go in a  safe and he goes home defenseless and is  not allowed to have a weapon at home how  should the Supreme Court role well the  Supreme Court should first recognize  that the Second Amendment guarantees an  individual right that belongs to you and  I ordinary members the American  community to have arms they should also  recognize that handguns as a class of  weapons  clearly fall within the type of arms  they're protected by the Second  Amendment and they should strike down  both the handgun ban as well as the  functional firearms ban how will the  Supreme Court role and the and the  rolling is due at any day any day now  certainly by the end of June 2008 is the  expectation well it's hard to predict  prognosticate the Supreme Court but the  argument went very well I thought at  least it seemed like the court was  conversant with the ideas it was a court  that had had read up on the issue and  and seemed open to to recognizing Second  Amendment rights so I'm optimistic that  the court will reach the correct  decision if the Supreme Court strikes  down the DC gun ban that does not  necessarily mean that the gun gun  control will go away or they could also  tailor it so that it only affects DC is  that right well yes that's true in both  grants first of all we don't have any  absolute rights under the United States  Constitution this will not be the end of  gun control and gun regulation but it  will put a stop to gun laws or it should  put a stop to gun laws that the  government cannot demonstrate an actual  need for and one which is respectful of  individual rights and so I think it  should put the brakes and should impact  certain gun control laws the ones are at  least the least defensible as far as the  laws impact we sued Washington DC  because it's a federal entity we don't  have to deal with the stickier issue of  whether or not the Second Amendment also  binds the state governments that will  have to be left for a future litigation  who who was the most receptive or did  you sense was the most receptive justice  to your arguments when you did oral  arguments it's difficult for me to  comment on individual justices but I  would say that many of the justices  seemed receptive to the idea that the  Second Amendment is a meaningful  individual right and you know there seem  to be broad support on the court at  least as far as we experienced the  argument you're not basing that on nods  because that could just be that they  were falling asleep no one fell asleep  during the argument it was quite a  lively argument and in fact the audio  transcript doesn't really capture the  the full spirit of the event but no it  seemed like the court was open to the  idea and it was it was quite open to a  national what who is your favorite the  Supreme Court justice currently sitting  on the bench I'd like to let you know  that in a few weeks once they will and  your least favorite I suspect we'll have  to wait for that I like them all talk a  little bit about your intellectual life  you and your professional career because  you're 37 so you're pretty young to be  arguing in front of the Supreme Court  you really can you can only go downhill  from here  so try enjoy you know have before that  happens you know tell us a little bit  about where you're from and where what's  your ideological background which are  intellectual heritage sure well I was  born in Israel but I grew up in Los  Angeles California I've always had  libertarian leanings I can't think of  any sort of epiphany or event or issue  that suddenly turned me on to  libertarian philosophy but it's the way  I approach the world I believe that  people should generally be free we mean  government government does have a role  in society but it's a role which which  needs to be reduced from its current  state what happens if you lose the the  case well I hope we don't lose this case  but if we lose the case and that will be  a teaching moment for America I mean we  are gonna have to come to terms with the  fact that that we are losing our rights  at an alarming rate the Fourth Amendment  jurisprudence from the court has been  troubling we've had an executive who has  really quite interesting and expansive  ideas of his authority I think sometimes  administration forgets that the  president is not the commander in chief  of the country he's only the commander  in chief or the Armed Forces I I think  that it's time that people take liberty  seriously we do have a wonderful  Constitution a wonderful Bill of Rights  it protects you know a vast array of  personal freedom and it's it's time I  hope that this case will will sort of  lead us in the other direction perhaps  get courts and judges to reflect  or and and give way to and meaning to  our freedoms Thank You Alan very much  thanks for having me I'm Nick Gillespie  for Reason TV thanks for watching
 

